WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
Today is Monday, September 10th, and this is a show with Rabbi Jose Faur.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.000
All right. How are you doing?

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Welcome to our lecture this evening.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:31.000
What we tried to accomplish was to bring together some young people to the old synagogue.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:43.000
The reason for doing that was to be able to spark within them, particularly at this time of the year, the little quenches of memory.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:01:07.100
And memory is something that is extremely important to me, to our speaker tonight, and is at the very root of what it is that we are trying to accomplish intellectually, morally, ethically, to go back to try and understand who we were to define who we are.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:19.100
In our speaker's work, we learn of rabbis hundreds of years old who are still fresh today,

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:30.100
who can still teach us, whose voice can still penetrate into our mind and into our soul.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:40.480
Rabbi Jose Faor has been a distinguished Rabbi, academic, philosopher, professor,

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:45.860
you name it, for over 40 years in our community.

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:48.860
For many of you he needs no introduction.

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:54.860
His pedigree, the places he studied, the places he's taught,

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:58.860
have all been for the benefit of our Sepharadi people

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:02.060
and continue to resonate to this day.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:05.980
He is one of the few individuals in our community

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:08.780
who has published extensively,

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:13.180
I would say over a hundred articles,

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:18.000
books that I actually brought with me.

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:22.880
This is the library, many of which are worn, as you can see.

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:26.800
Books in Hebrew, books in English,

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:32.040
even a book in Spanish that was written many, many years ago when he was at university in Spain.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:44.980
And the basic idea in his work is to begin to understand how we were formed as a people

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:47.460
and what that means to us.

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:53.060
In the lecture tonight, I hope that many of you who are familiar with his work

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:59.040
get even more benefit from your understanding of what he said in the past.

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:01.000
And for those of you who have never heard him speak,

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:05.600
I hope that you hear things that you've never heard before.

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:08.320
In terms of the way that we are educated,

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:13.360
I believe that the main purpose of education is for self-knowledge.

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:18.500
It's for people to understand who they are as human beings and where they come from.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:26.260
What we tried to do again with this lecture was to have people actually sit in seats that

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:33.260
people sat in many, many years ago, grandparents, great-grandparents, to begin the process of

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:36.260
trying to understand who we are.

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:42.260
In my own personal experience, the Rabbi has been a tremendous resource in my life, an

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:47.460
an overwhelming resource in terms of the ideas that I have, the work that I've done,

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:51.080
and it's a truly humbling experience to be in the synagogue

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:54.320
where my grandfather was once a committee member

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:58.220
and introduce Rabbi Jose Fargo.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:04.600
Thank you very much.

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:06.860
I would say already,

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:17.180
something about our Tora

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:33.020
we believe that our Tora is eternal but eternity in the Jewish mind is not like a speck of some

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:45.660
sort of a rock in space that turns over the same without changing. Eternity in our mind

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:57.680
means that the Tora is meaningful in every situation. It doesn't mean that it's the same

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:01.960
because the situation did not change.

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:07.360
What the eternity of the Tora is, is a challenge.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:14.620
How do you find the Tora in our present situation,

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:18.240
whether the present is a thousand years from now,

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:25.140
or a thousand years ago, and yet the Tora is meaningful?

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:38.140
Before I come to the main subject and to tell you why I think that the Tora is particularly significant today for us in this United States of America,

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:40.840
let me point something about the past.

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:53.460
Whenever we spoke about the Tora in the past, 100, 200, 500, 1000 years ago, we spoke about the philosophical dimension of Tora.

00:05:53.460 --> 00:06:07.060
how meaningful it is. There is one God, we have to prove it. If there is one God, then there is harmony in nature, physics, metaphysics.

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:22.840
Later on, we speak about the ethics of the Tora. Then we speak about the historical view, how the Tora sees, perceives, projects history.

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:32.840
But there is another dimension in the Tora that our parents, they could never speak about.

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:40.840
And perhaps that dimension is the most significant dimension of the Tora.

00:06:40.840 --> 00:07:03.360
Every culture has taboos. Certain subjects you're not supposed to speak about. And one of the dimensions of the Tora had been a taboo. Something which is prohibited to speak about.

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:06.380
since the Hurban Bet HaMikdash

00:07:06.380 --> 00:07:09.080
nobody would dare speak

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:11.180
about that subject

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:12.080
of the Tora

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:15.100
but because we are today

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:18.500
in new millennium

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:21.000
and we are fortunate

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:22.060
it's a privilege

00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:24.900
to be living in this

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:26.660
United States of America

00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:29.320
we could begin

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:30.420
to explore

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:31.900
something

00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:47.880
that even people like Haram Bamb, they dare only to hush about it, but not really to analyze in a clear and methodological way.

00:07:47.880 --> 00:08:01.680
What is the subject? The Tora basically has two dimensions. One dimension is between us and God. Everybody knows this.

00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:16.500
Tefillin, Kasher, Shabbat, Kiddush, all this. But the Tora is also a political, has a political dimension.

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:32.300
When the Tora speaks about Erez Israel, it's not only a piece of geography, it's basically a political organization.

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:48.560
The Tora not only speaks about Tefillin, it speaks about Kitth Selam El Hama, the laws of war, Shofetim Reshotelim, the judges, the police force, the Melekh, the taxes.

00:08:48.560 --> 00:09:08.560
The Tora speaks about politics. When you pray in the Amirah and say, Boney Yerushalayim, and you are pointing towards Jerusalem, you are making a political statement.

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:12.000
you're saying something very important

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:13.640
one day

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:16.300
Jerusalem will be built

00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:18.740
and I shall return

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:20.540
well, one minute

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:23.560
again, Jerusalem shall be built

00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:25.900
and I shall return

00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:27.500
this is a political statement

00:09:27.500 --> 00:09:29.200
why?

00:09:30.260 --> 00:09:31.300
number one

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:32.680
you're a citizen

00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:35.900
of this United States

00:09:35.900 --> 00:09:38.140
we say we're in Galut

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:43.940
But how could you be in exile if you are a citizen of a country?

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:47.500
What is the political philosophy behind it?

00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:53.600
You say, I'm going to abandon this country and go to Jerusalem?

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:56.300
This is a political statement.

00:09:56.780 --> 00:09:59.520
There are some political notions behind it.

00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:01.200
What is it?

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:05.860
when you say the Messiah shall come

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:08.640
and save us

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:16.880
does an American citizen

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:17.780
need

00:10:17.780 --> 00:10:20.620
no but I'm Jewish

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:23.060
you're Jewish but you're an American citizen

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:24.720
you say that you're going to be

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:25.760
goel

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:26.960
how

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:30.760
you say

00:10:30.760 --> 00:10:44.060
that it was slated. I'm in Geneva, I'm a Swiss citizen, I'm a British Englishman in London,

00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:56.340
yes? American, French in Paris and say this year, Paris, London, Geneva,

00:10:56.340 --> 00:11:06.300
a Tzuri. I'm there, I'm a Eivet, I'm a slave. I'm going to be in Israel and I'm going to

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:14.760
be free. We are the only Americans who are not free. What is the political thought behind

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:29.760
it. To be a Jew means not only to have our own notion about theology which we have, ethics,

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:38.540
philosophy, but first and foremost to have a political philosophy. And the question is

00:11:38.540 --> 00:11:42.500
What is the political philosophy of the Jewish people?

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:43.680
What is it?

00:11:45.080 --> 00:11:50.980
Now, 50 years ago, we cannot ask this question even in America.

00:11:53.060 --> 00:12:00.340
100 years ago, certainly not in Syria, or in Turkey, or in Egypt, or in Poland, or in Russia.

00:12:01.220 --> 00:12:05.280
We cannot speak about the political philosophy of the Jewish people.

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:13.280
But today we are privileged. We are really privileged people.

00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:25.280
Never before Jews outside Erez Israel enjoyed not only the political freedom,

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:29.280
but the intellectual freedom to ask questions.

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:34.280
Today we could ask questions. Today we could open books.

00:12:34.280 --> 00:12:52.040
Today we could read and publish what is the political philosophy of the Jewish people. Now, I am privileged in a certain sense because I teach two subjects that I love.

00:12:52.040 --> 00:13:00.040
I teach Talmud at Bar-Iran, that is paradise for me and my students also.

00:13:00.040 --> 00:13:06.040
And I teach law at the law school of Netanya.

00:13:06.040 --> 00:13:10.040
And I teach them what they call in Hebrew, Mishpat Ibri.

00:13:10.040 --> 00:13:18.040
Those aspects of Jewish law, of Jewish halakha, that are part and parcel of the Israeli legal system.

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:23.880
for instance, family law, inheritance law, child support, divorce, all these type of things.

00:13:24.680 --> 00:13:36.140
And therefore, I was able to penetrate and to express in a more elegant system

00:13:36.140 --> 00:13:41.320
what is the philosophy, the legal philosophy, the political philosophy of Israel.

00:13:41.580 --> 00:13:44.200
Let's begin with the very beginning.

00:13:44.200 --> 00:14:07.160
We have in America a constitution. Now, the constitution is the basis of this country, alright? But you and me have no right to give an authoritative interpretation of the constitution.

00:14:07.160 --> 00:14:31.060
Not only I, you, we cannot give an authoritative interpretation of the Constitution, even the courts cannot explain it. Only the Supreme Court of the United States can tell us what is the legal dimension of the Constitution.

00:14:31.060 --> 00:14:48.060
Fine. Very clear. Let's go into Tora. We have a written law, that's the Tora, and we have an oral law.

00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:51.160
the word Tora

00:14:51.160 --> 00:14:53.920
when the Jews who wrote

00:14:53.920 --> 00:14:54.520
Greek

00:14:54.520 --> 00:14:57.860
in Alexandria 2200

00:14:57.860 --> 00:14:59.900
years ago had to translate

00:14:59.900 --> 00:15:00.760
the word Tora

00:15:00.760 --> 00:15:03.220
they translated as

00:15:03.220 --> 00:15:04.360
nomos

00:15:04.360 --> 00:15:08.060
what does it mean the word nomos

00:15:08.060 --> 00:15:09.780
in Greek

00:15:09.780 --> 00:15:11.880
exactly

00:15:11.880 --> 00:15:13.040
what the word Sharia

00:15:13.040 --> 00:15:15.320
means in Arabic

00:15:15.320 --> 00:15:17.820
and what does the

00:15:17.820 --> 00:15:26.780
word Sharia means in Arabic. Exactly what the word Lex means in Latin. And what the

00:15:26.780 --> 00:15:38.520
word Lex means in Latin. Exactly what the word Loi in French and Lo in English means.

00:15:38.520 --> 00:15:46.020
Properly defined, nomos is the constitution of the Jewish people.

00:15:46.020 --> 00:15:49.360
We have a constitution.

00:15:50.440 --> 00:15:56.520
The constitution of the Jewish people is there in the Sefer Tora.

00:15:57.260 --> 00:16:02.240
The Sefer Tora is the constitution of the Jewish people.

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:05.180
It is a binding document.

00:16:06.520 --> 00:16:07.880
Who could interpret it?

00:16:08.520 --> 00:16:10.260
Ah, I could interpret it.

00:16:10.980 --> 00:16:15.160
And I have the same right to interpret the Hommash, the Tora,

00:16:15.860 --> 00:16:18.700
as I have the right to interpret the Constitution.

00:16:19.380 --> 00:16:21.500
I could give a very intelligent interpretation,

00:16:22.180 --> 00:16:25.700
but it has no legal bearing.

00:16:26.740 --> 00:16:31.420
The only one who could give the proper interpretation of the Tora

00:16:31.420 --> 00:16:35.840
is the equivalent of the Supreme Court of America.

00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:39.620
And what is the supreme court of the Jewish people?

00:16:40.680 --> 00:16:42.420
The Sanhedrin.

00:16:43.920 --> 00:16:48.820
And who was the first head of the Sanhedrin?

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:52.160
Moshe Rabbenu.

00:16:52.760 --> 00:16:57.700
Moshe Rabbenu constituted the first Sanhedrin.

00:16:58.280 --> 00:16:59.480
The Shivim Zekanim.

00:17:00.740 --> 00:17:04.200
Is that interpretation legally binding?

00:17:04.200 --> 00:17:05.400
Yes.

00:17:05.840 --> 00:17:09.780
Because they are the Supreme Court of Israel.

00:17:10.700 --> 00:17:13.300
By the way, do you know why 70 people?

00:17:14.540 --> 00:17:16.740
Why there is 70 people in the Supreme Court of Israel?

00:17:17.760 --> 00:17:18.280
Why?

00:17:20.680 --> 00:17:24.180
You're not going to hear it from anybody else.

00:17:25.500 --> 00:17:31.840
Because when we left Egypt, how many families we were?

00:17:34.040 --> 00:17:34.700
How many?

00:17:34.700 --> 00:18:01.660
No, when we left Egypt. How many families? No, no, 58 families. We were 58 families. 600,000 people coming from 58 families. That's why we have 70 members in the Sanhedrin. Why?

00:18:01.660 --> 00:18:19.360
Every family has one delegate and the tribes, 12 tribes, we have 70. 58 representing the families plus 12 representing each of it.

00:18:19.360 --> 00:18:44.180
That is the basis of a true democracy. Now, very important, with all due respect, Yeshua, the head of the Christian religion, the founder of the religion, he represents the incarnation of the Logos.

00:18:44.180 --> 00:18:53.460
Muhammad, he's a cosmocrator. He's going to be a general who's going to be like Napoleon or Hitler undefeated.

00:18:53.460 --> 00:18:56.780
Going to surrender, everybody's going to surrender under the sword of Muhammad.

00:18:57.340 --> 00:19:03.560
Yes, Musharrab Benu is not a logos, some sort of an angel.

00:19:04.880 --> 00:19:11.260
He's not Napoleon. He is the head of the Jewish court.

00:19:11.260 --> 00:19:31.760
He is the founder of the legal system. So, very clearly, we have a written law, the constitution, we have a supreme court, going all the way up to the Talmud.

00:19:31.760 --> 00:19:35.420
and these laws

00:19:35.420 --> 00:19:38.420
are the basis

00:19:38.420 --> 00:19:42.740
of something which has no parallel in history

00:19:42.740 --> 00:19:45.500
the Jewish nation

00:19:45.500 --> 00:19:47.500
let me explain how it works

00:19:47.500 --> 00:19:52.580
in America

00:19:52.580 --> 00:19:54.180
the greatest democracy

00:19:54.180 --> 00:19:56.400
by the way

00:19:56.400 --> 00:19:59.340
since I lived in Israel

00:19:59.340 --> 00:20:02.580
I came to appreciate more and more America.

00:20:02.960 --> 00:20:04.320
Alright? We have a great nation.

00:20:05.380 --> 00:20:07.140
So I want you to know that.

00:20:07.720 --> 00:20:13.260
But what is the basis of the Constitution of America?

00:20:14.080 --> 00:20:18.880
What makes this Constitution binding on me and you?

00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:20.720
Something very interesting.

00:20:21.500 --> 00:20:26.560
The Constitution of America, like the Magna Carta of England,

00:20:26.560 --> 00:20:33.500
like they called Napoleon in France, etc. etc.

00:20:34.320 --> 00:20:36.580
It's based upon one single concept.

00:20:37.600 --> 00:20:38.000
Might.

00:20:41.000 --> 00:20:42.040
Might.

00:20:42.040 --> 00:20:45.540
There is a sovereign.

00:20:47.100 --> 00:20:51.120
There is a sovereign by virtue of its might.

00:20:51.120 --> 00:20:54.920
and that sovereign

00:20:54.920 --> 00:20:57.560
institutes

00:20:57.560 --> 00:20:59.500
a legal system.

00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:02.040
A quiz.

00:21:03.620 --> 00:21:05.480
Who, which president

00:21:05.480 --> 00:21:07.320
in the United States was never

00:21:07.320 --> 00:21:07.820
elected?

00:21:10.180 --> 00:21:10.940
Who?

00:21:13.240 --> 00:21:15.340
No, no. He was elected by

00:21:15.340 --> 00:21:17.340
being a vice president. When you elect

00:21:17.340 --> 00:21:19.060
a president, automatically

00:21:19.060 --> 00:21:20.720
you elect the vice president.

00:21:21.120 --> 00:21:26.780
all right then the Senate makes a difference he's elected by a Senate

00:21:26.780 --> 00:21:32.620
Washington George Washington is very interesting our first and beloved really

00:21:32.620 --> 00:21:37.620
was of Israel I have nothing against him I think that he was from Hasidic

00:21:37.620 --> 00:21:45.440
Motawlam I really truly loved George Washington but he was not elected what

00:21:45.440 --> 00:21:56.000
What were the credentials, what were the legal basis to appoint him president?

00:21:56.000 --> 00:22:02.460
The fact that he defeated the British.

00:22:02.460 --> 00:22:14.000
In other words, sword, might is the basis of political legitimacy.

00:22:14.000 --> 00:22:27.140
is the basis of our Tora? And here we have something that nobody else has. The

00:22:27.140 --> 00:22:39.380
basis of our Tora was that when we left Egypt and we passed on our way, we stopped

00:22:39.380 --> 00:22:50.900
at Mount Sinai, God revealed Himself and He proposed and we accepted a contract.

00:22:52.100 --> 00:23:02.500
How do you call in saying Hebrew contract? Berit. A berit was proposed by God.

00:23:02.500 --> 00:23:19.260
we, the Jewish people, accept it and the contract is the Tora. To put it in a different way.

00:23:19.260 --> 00:23:49.000
in pagan political thought. Might is right because we are mighty. We are right. What gives, for instance, the people in former Yugoslavia, you know, the Atlantic things to come there and so on, and we cannot do it because they are mighty. Yes?

00:23:49.260 --> 00:24:11.980
Might is right. Yes? Why America could go to Vietnam and we cannot go to Ramallah. Why? Might is right. Yes? Why nobody asks, well, you know, you preach this, this, the natives, what about the American Indians?

00:24:11.980 --> 00:24:22.980
Might is right. In the Jewish mind, right is might. Because we have a Tora that we

00:24:22.980 --> 00:24:30.380
have accepted, then we have the right and the basis of the Tora to appoint

00:24:30.380 --> 00:24:39.220
Shofetim, Shoterim, eventually we could appoint the king. Yes, but the basis of

00:24:39.220 --> 00:24:46.720
political authority is the Tora. Let me put it in another way. Because there is a

00:24:46.720 --> 00:24:55.740
monarch in the Goy world, there is a law. Law is the will of the monarch, the will

00:24:55.740 --> 00:25:03.060
the will of the sovereign. All right? In Judaism is exactly the opposite. Because we

00:25:03.060 --> 00:25:10.060
have a law, then we could have a sovereign, then we could have authority. The basis is

00:25:10.280 --> 00:25:17.280
the law. And because the basis is the law, not the other way around, you can talk about

00:25:18.980 --> 00:25:25.980
a very interesting situation. In international law, you conquer a nation, might, that nation

00:25:25.980 --> 00:25:42.300
might, that nation can no longer defend its territory, that nation ceases to exist. Montenegro

00:25:42.300 --> 00:25:52.140
doesn't exist, correct? Turkestan doesn't exist, yes? Why? They were conquered. The

00:25:52.140 --> 00:26:02.040
do not exist. Yes? Why? They were conquered. Might is right and therefore when a nation

00:26:02.040 --> 00:26:10.900
does not have the might, the power to control its territory, it does not exist. We say the

00:26:10.900 --> 00:26:21.240
other way around. Although they conquer our territory, what makes us a nation is

00:26:21.240 --> 00:26:32.640
is not our military capability to defend a given boundaries, but the law.

00:26:32.640 --> 00:26:35.000
Because we have a law, we are a nation.

00:26:35.000 --> 00:26:41.680
Therefore, although we were expelled from our territory,

00:26:41.680 --> 00:26:47.440
still we do exist as a nation.

00:26:47.440 --> 00:26:48.780
It's very interesting.

00:26:48.780 --> 00:26:56.680
Returning back to Israel means simply that we never cease to exist as a nation.

00:26:57.340 --> 00:26:58.240
Why not?

00:26:58.240 --> 00:27:06.380
Because we are a nation by virtue of our law, our constitution.

00:27:08.440 --> 00:27:16.680
And that constitution is what gives us a right over the piece of land and not the other way around.

